<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Ndongo</h1><p class="st-meta">1500 CE – 1671 CE (approx.) · capital Kabasa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Mbundu kingdom of the Ngola, whose title gave Angola its name and whose queen Njinga fought the Portuguese for decades.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Ndongo was formed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mbundu">Mbundu</a> chiefdoms between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwanza-river">Kwanza</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucala-river">Lukala</a> rivers in the late 15th century under a ruler titled the Ngola. Initially a tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kongo">Kingdom of Kongo</a>, it asserted independence in the 1550s, and a Portuguese embassy reached its capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabasa">Kabasa</a> in 1560.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Ngola ruled through regional lords (sobas) who paid tribute in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, salt, cloth and slaves. From 1575, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paulo-dias-de-novais">Paulo Dias de Novais</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luanda">Luanda</a>, Ndongo faced constant war with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/portuguese-angola">Portuguese Angola</a> and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imbangala">Imbangala</a> allies, who sought slaves for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic trade</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/njinga-of-ndongo-and-matamba">Njinga Mbande</a>, who became ruler in 1624, negotiated, converted, fought and allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>, later ruling from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-matamba">Kingdom of Matamba</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Ndongo was split between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/njinga-of-ndongo-and-matamba">Njinga</a>'s party and a Portuguese-backed puppet king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngola-hari">Ngola Hari</a>. When his successor rebelled, the Portuguese stormed the fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pungo-andongo">Pungo Andongo</a> in 1671, ending the kingdom. The Ngola title gave <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/portuguese-angola">Angola</a> its name, and Njinga is a national heroine.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-ndongo.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Ndongo" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Samhanin · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Kingdom_of_Ndongo.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1500 CE – 1671 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kabasa</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ngola Kiluanje kia Ndambi, Njinga Mbande, Ngola Hari</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Traditional Mbundu religion, Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Kimbundu</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/angola">Angola</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ndongo, Ngola</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1586&amp;focus=kingdom-of-ndongo" aria-label="Kingdom of Ndongo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5171 2645 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Ndongo lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5395 2668h0M5366 2764h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5491 2901h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5491 2901h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Angola. <a href="/atlas?year=1586&amp;focus=kingdom-of-ndongo">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

